WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.230 --> 00:00:04.170
Today, I'm really excited to
be joined by my close friend

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:07.500
and colleague Joshua Angrist,
a professor of economics also

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:12.060
at MIT, and newly minted Nobel
Prize winner for his work

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:14.580
on natural experiments
in economics,

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:16.800
and by our colleague
Clémence Idoux,

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:21.460
a researcher at Blueprint
Labs and an MIT PhD graduate.

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:23.500
Today's talk will be
pretty wide ranging.

00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:26.350
But we'll focus mainly on
Blueprint Labs research

00:00:26.350 --> 00:00:29.080
into admissions policies
in public schools

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:33.160
and their impacts on integration
and student achievement.

00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:36.310
I'll introduce Josh and Clémence
and get into our discussion

00:00:36.310 --> 00:00:37.670
in just a moment.

00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:40.840
But first, let me make a
few housekeeping notes.

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:43.990
I'll ask Josh and Clémence each
to tell us a little bit about

00:00:43.990 --> 00:00:45.460
themselves and their work.

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:48.130
And then, we'll get into a
discussion about their research

00:00:48.130 --> 00:00:50.680
and its implications,
what they've learned,

00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:55.060
recommendations for
educators, and so on.

00:00:55.060 --> 00:00:57.040
For the last 15
minutes or so, we'll

00:00:57.040 --> 00:01:00.440
open it up to audience
Q&amp;A. At any time,

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:03.040
please feel free to put
questions for our panelists

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:04.540
into the chat.

00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:06.820
Our moderators today,
Janine and Catherine,

00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:08.620
will be collecting
all of the questions

00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:11.620
for us to go through
during the Q&amp;A.

00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:14.230
If you'd like to go on camera
and ask your question directly,

00:01:14.230 --> 00:01:16.870
please use the Raise
Hand function on Zoom,

00:01:16.870 --> 00:01:20.930
and a moderator will promote
you to panelists during the Q&amp;A.

00:01:20.930 --> 00:01:23.260
And if you have any questions
about any of this, please

00:01:23.260 --> 00:01:25.970
put them in the chat, and
the moderators will help you.

00:01:25.970 --> 00:01:28.960
So without further
ado, let's get started.

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:31.270
Josh, I'm going
to start with you.

00:01:31.270 --> 00:01:35.200
Josh Angrist is the Ford
Professor of Economics at MIT,

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:38.290
a co-founder and director
of MIT'S Blueprint Labs,

00:01:38.290 --> 00:01:40.330
and a research associate
at the National

00:01:40.330 --> 00:01:42.280
Bureau of Economic Research.

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:45.550
Josh, can you tell us
a bit about your work?

00:01:45.550 --> 00:01:46.660
I'd love to, Parag.

00:01:46.660 --> 00:01:49.270
Thanks so much for that very
flattering introduction.

00:01:49.270 --> 00:01:56.140
It's been an unusually exciting
semester for us at the lab.

00:01:56.140 --> 00:01:59.470
Usually, we have a lot
of excitement anyway.

00:01:59.470 --> 00:02:02.060
This has been extra
special this fall.

00:02:02.060 --> 00:02:03.760
Of course, I'm
thrilled and humbled

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:08.990
to have been recognized
with a Nobel Prize

00:02:08.990 --> 00:02:12.640
and to share the Prize
with my thesis advisor--

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:14.350
one of my thesis
advisors, Dave Card,

00:02:14.350 --> 00:02:17.770
and my long-time
collaborator Guido Imbens.

00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:20.950
I have a couple of fun pics
to share with our audience--

00:02:20.950 --> 00:02:24.430
let me see if I can
get that to work--

00:02:24.430 --> 00:02:27.020
from the Nobel experience.

00:02:27.020 --> 00:02:30.145
Let's see if that'll
come up on my iPad.

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:36.480
And so, what we
do in the lab is,

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:40.480
we estimate causal effects
of things related to schools.

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:42.900
So I think of that as
illuminating the path for cause

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:43.560
to effect.

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:46.840
And that's what the Prize
recognizes-- that kind of work.

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:49.380
So all of us at
Blueprint Labs, we

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:51.000
couldn't be more
pleased by this.

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:53.490
And it really is a
group effort at the lab.

00:02:53.490 --> 00:02:55.150
It's been a little disruptive.

00:02:55.150 --> 00:03:00.090
So that's what I came home
to early on October 11th--

00:03:00.090 --> 00:03:01.710
photographers in my driveway.

00:03:01.710 --> 00:03:03.510
I'm holding my granddaughter.

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:05.610
And that's my wife Mira there.

00:03:05.610 --> 00:03:09.090
And it's more or less
been like that ever since.

00:03:09.090 --> 00:03:12.060
I'm looking forward
to going back to work,

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:17.160
having a little coffee, and
getting up to a quieter day.

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:19.890
But it is inspiring
to get the Prize.

00:03:19.890 --> 00:03:23.410
There's an inspiring quote in
Steven Pinker's latest book,

00:03:23.410 --> 00:03:30.270
which I think reflects the way I
feel about the work that we do.

00:03:30.270 --> 00:03:32.160
I was surprised to
see this in the book.

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:35.190
And it means that we're sort
of really getting our tools out

00:03:35.190 --> 00:03:35.730
there.

00:03:35.730 --> 00:03:38.790
When a data scientist finds
a regression discontinuity

00:03:38.790 --> 00:03:41.860
or an instrumental variable,
it's a really good day.

00:03:41.860 --> 00:03:45.570
And I'm hoping that I've
made those days better

00:03:45.570 --> 00:03:52.140
for some people with the
work that I do at Blueprint.

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:56.010
I'm very lucky to have such
fine collaborators at the lab,

00:03:56.010 --> 00:04:00.630
like Parag and our
talented student Clémence.

00:04:00.630 --> 00:04:05.400
We've been working as a team
for over a decade on questions

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:06.660
related to schools.

00:04:06.660 --> 00:04:09.330
We work on school assignment--

00:04:09.330 --> 00:04:13.050
how kids choose and are
offered opportunities

00:04:13.050 --> 00:04:15.600
to attend different schools.

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:18.700
And we work on
school effectiveness,

00:04:18.700 --> 00:04:21.839
which we typically
measure by the learning

00:04:21.839 --> 00:04:24.480
gains or the effects
of school attendance

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:30.450
on how likely students are
to eventually go to college.

00:04:30.450 --> 00:04:32.860
These sound like fairly
quotidian matters.

00:04:32.860 --> 00:04:37.170
But as you'll soon see in
Clémence's presentation--

00:04:37.170 --> 00:04:39.450
and she's really the
star of the show today--

00:04:39.450 --> 00:04:41.490
this is the heart of
the matter for one

00:04:41.490 --> 00:04:43.920
of the most controversial
and important questions

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:45.700
in American public life.

00:04:45.700 --> 00:04:49.350
And that's the question
of integration--

00:04:49.350 --> 00:04:53.790
racial integration in schools,
and what sort of policies

00:04:53.790 --> 00:04:57.760
facilitate integration, and what
sort of policies inhibit it.

00:04:57.760 --> 00:05:00.750
And closely related to
that is the question

00:05:00.750 --> 00:05:03.750
of school effectiveness--
whether opportunities

00:05:03.750 --> 00:05:07.590
to attend different schools
indeed promote learning

00:05:07.590 --> 00:05:10.710
and lead to better outcomes.

00:05:10.710 --> 00:05:13.680
School quality for many people
is really life-changing.

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:18.240
And I like to make this point
by mentioning a paper that David

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:21.150
Card-- who's one of
the Prize winners--

00:05:21.150 --> 00:05:23.220
the Nobel that I share--

00:05:23.220 --> 00:05:27.000
wrote with our late friend
Alan Krueger in the early '90s.

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:30.480
They showed that improvements
in school quality

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:34.260
really explains a
substantial fraction

00:05:34.260 --> 00:05:39.330
of the gains in earnings
of Black men in the 1960s

00:05:39.330 --> 00:05:40.020
and '70s.

00:05:40.020 --> 00:05:44.400
And there was great progress
in terms of the racial earnings

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:46.320
gap in those decades--

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:48.520
not complete convergence
by any means.

00:05:48.520 --> 00:05:51.150
We have a long way to go--
but progress nonetheless.

00:05:51.150 --> 00:05:54.270
And a lot of that progress is
explained by increasing school

00:05:54.270 --> 00:05:59.070
quality and increases in
the quality of the schools

00:05:59.070 --> 00:06:03.090
that Black men attend.

00:06:03.090 --> 00:06:06.780
We focus on men typically in
research from that period,

00:06:06.780 --> 00:06:09.210
because female labor
force participation

00:06:09.210 --> 00:06:11.580
rates were relatively low.

00:06:11.580 --> 00:06:16.590
In any case, the topic
today is certainly

00:06:16.590 --> 00:06:21.030
interesting, and exciting,
and relevant to current policy

00:06:21.030 --> 00:06:22.530
debates.

00:06:22.530 --> 00:06:25.110
Parag, Clémence, and I
have uncovered all sorts

00:06:25.110 --> 00:06:28.320
of interesting findings
related to the effects of going

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:32.220
to schools of different types in
Boston, Chicago, and New York.

00:06:32.220 --> 00:06:36.660
And as part of her thesis work,
Clémence has taken a deep dive

00:06:36.660 --> 00:06:41.550
into the consequences of an
unprecedented change in New

00:06:41.550 --> 00:06:46.590
York City assignment policies in
an effort to reduce segregation

00:06:46.590 --> 00:06:50.310
in two of the city's
highest income areas.

00:06:50.310 --> 00:06:53.610
They're called districts,
because New York is so big.

00:06:53.610 --> 00:06:56.580
Even smaller parts of New York
are called school districts.

00:06:56.580 --> 00:07:00.060
In any case, I'll hand off now
to Clémence and let her tell

00:07:00.060 --> 00:07:02.780
this remarkable story.

00:07:02.780 --> 00:07:03.740
Great, Josh.

00:07:03.740 --> 00:07:05.180
Thank you so much.

00:07:05.180 --> 00:07:09.040
Clémence, the show is yours.

00:07:09.040 --> 00:07:11.300
Thank you for the introduction.

00:07:11.300 --> 00:07:15.250
Let me share some slides that
I can show you some pictures

00:07:15.250 --> 00:07:16.060
as well--

00:07:16.060 --> 00:07:19.450
that won't be as funny as
Joshua here, I'm afraid.

00:07:19.450 --> 00:07:22.410
But that's hard to beat.

00:07:22.410 --> 00:07:26.240
And so, like as I
think, Josh said,

00:07:26.240 --> 00:07:29.680
I received my PhD
from MIT this year.

00:07:29.680 --> 00:07:31.520
And now, I'm a
post-doctoral fellow

00:07:31.520 --> 00:07:35.650
at MIT Department of
Economics and Blueprint Labs.

00:07:35.650 --> 00:07:38.770
And so during my PhD,
I focused my research

00:07:38.770 --> 00:07:41.830
on understanding what are
the consequences of school

00:07:41.830 --> 00:07:45.550
assignment rules on student
achievement and school

00:07:45.550 --> 00:07:46.220
diversity.

00:07:46.220 --> 00:07:48.220
So what I mean is
whether you can

00:07:48.220 --> 00:07:50.230
design a system of
school admission

00:07:50.230 --> 00:07:53.440
that can increase school
diversity or increase test

00:07:53.440 --> 00:07:55.960
scores.

00:07:55.960 --> 00:07:58.570
Today, I will focus on
my work in New York City.

00:07:58.570 --> 00:08:00.400
So I've been
studying the past two

00:08:00.400 --> 00:08:05.050
years, the consequences of New
York City integration policies.

00:08:05.050 --> 00:08:07.330
And as the starting
point from this project

00:08:07.330 --> 00:08:10.990
was the observation that
American school districts are

00:08:10.990 --> 00:08:14.380
still very much segregated
although more than almost

00:08:14.380 --> 00:08:18.670
70 years have elapsed since
Brown v Board of Education,

00:08:18.670 --> 00:08:22.510
which is, as you may know,
the Supreme Court ruling that

00:08:22.510 --> 00:08:26.320
ordered all school districts
across the US to desegregate.

00:08:26.320 --> 00:08:31.270
So to give you a number,
roughly 17% of American schools

00:08:31.270 --> 00:08:36.010
today enroll a student body
that is approximately 75% Black

00:08:36.010 --> 00:08:37.059
or Hispanic students.

00:08:37.059 --> 00:08:40.570
These are a lot of discrepancy
on the student body

00:08:40.570 --> 00:08:42.820
of different schools in the US.

00:08:42.820 --> 00:08:47.080
And this has driven a
lot of media attention.

00:08:47.080 --> 00:08:51.220
And when the public debate
talks about this question,

00:08:51.220 --> 00:08:56.200
it often focuses on the question
of racial differences in access

00:08:56.200 --> 00:08:58.390
to selective enrollment schools.

00:08:58.390 --> 00:09:00.070
So selective
enrollment schools are

00:09:00.070 --> 00:09:03.550
schools that select students
based on past achievement

00:09:03.550 --> 00:09:05.590
or other characteristics.

00:09:05.590 --> 00:09:07.000
I'd like to cite some.

00:09:07.000 --> 00:09:09.220
For instance, a free
exam school in Boston.

00:09:09.220 --> 00:09:11.200
I typically selected
enrollment schools

00:09:11.200 --> 00:09:16.000
because they select people
based on the result at an exam.

00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:18.790
During the COVID-19
pandemic, testing

00:09:18.790 --> 00:09:21.190
was interrupted in
many cities in the US.

00:09:21.190 --> 00:09:24.580
And as a result, there
was an increased interest

00:09:24.580 --> 00:09:26.200
in screened school access.

00:09:26.200 --> 00:09:29.290
And this is reflected
by some articles I've

00:09:29.290 --> 00:09:31.540
pulled over in the screen here.

00:09:31.540 --> 00:09:35.710
And for instance, in Virginia,
the first high school board

00:09:35.710 --> 00:09:37.880
decided to eliminate
admission test

00:09:37.880 --> 00:09:40.880
to its very selective and
prestigious high schools--

00:09:40.880 --> 00:09:43.840
the Thomas Jefferson
High School-- last year.

00:09:43.840 --> 00:09:48.370
In Boston, so just on the other
side of the river from here,

00:09:48.370 --> 00:09:52.300
BPS have enacted two
successive admissions reforms

00:09:52.300 --> 00:09:53.710
in the past two years.

00:09:53.710 --> 00:09:56.500
And this admission
reform targeted

00:09:56.500 --> 00:09:58.820
the city free exam schools.

00:09:58.820 --> 00:10:01.810
So this free school in
Boston that selects student

00:10:01.810 --> 00:10:02.680
based on an exam.

00:10:02.680 --> 00:10:06.190
Those are upper middle
school and high school.

00:10:06.190 --> 00:10:08.860
They select student
from seventh grade on.

00:10:08.860 --> 00:10:11.980
And so, as a city, the
two successive rounds

00:10:11.980 --> 00:10:14.330
of admissions reform
was slightly different.

00:10:14.330 --> 00:10:19.180
But basically, the idea was to
give some preferential access

00:10:19.180 --> 00:10:24.190
to this very prestigious
school to students from lower--

00:10:24.190 --> 00:10:26.110
from disadvantaged backgrounds.

00:10:26.110 --> 00:10:29.860
The city created some system
of priority for these students

00:10:29.860 --> 00:10:32.440
so that they would get
better access to these very

00:10:32.440 --> 00:10:34.030
prestigious schools.

00:10:34.030 --> 00:10:39.940
In New York City, finally, last
year the New York City school

00:10:39.940 --> 00:10:45.160
board decided to suspend
academic screen for admission

00:10:45.160 --> 00:10:47.410
at its middle school for a year.

00:10:47.410 --> 00:10:50.510
And the suspension has been
extended for another year

00:10:50.510 --> 00:10:53.320
this year, since the
city didn't have any test

00:10:53.320 --> 00:10:55.210
score to base admission on.

00:10:55.210 --> 00:10:58.930
So there has been this
very temporary suspension

00:10:58.930 --> 00:11:02.800
of screening in middle schools.

00:11:02.800 --> 00:11:07.210
So just to say, there has
been this increased interest

00:11:07.210 --> 00:11:10.750
in this issue of whether
these selective admissions

00:11:10.750 --> 00:11:13.540
policies are contributing
to segregation

00:11:13.540 --> 00:11:16.180
in American schools.

00:11:16.180 --> 00:11:18.850
New York City is quite
an interesting place

00:11:18.850 --> 00:11:20.050
to study this question.

00:11:20.050 --> 00:11:22.300
Because first, it's the
larger school districts

00:11:22.300 --> 00:11:23.270
in the United States.

00:11:23.270 --> 00:11:25.420
So the district,
as you may know,

00:11:25.420 --> 00:11:28.270
enroll roughly one million
of students every year.

00:11:28.270 --> 00:11:32.620
And that's approximately
0.3% of the US population.

00:11:32.620 --> 00:11:34.480
The district is also
deemed one of the most

00:11:34.480 --> 00:11:36.160
segregated in the country.

00:11:36.160 --> 00:11:40.420
So to give you a number, 50%
of schools in New York City

00:11:40.420 --> 00:11:45.280
enroll more than 90% of
Black and Hispanic students

00:11:45.280 --> 00:11:48.260
that are also eligible for
free and reduced price lunch.

00:11:48.260 --> 00:11:52.300
That is a vast disparity
of the student population

00:11:52.300 --> 00:11:55.180
enrollment across schools.

00:11:55.180 --> 00:11:57.130
New York City is
also interesting,

00:11:57.130 --> 00:12:00.070
because it's a city in
which screen schools are

00:12:00.070 --> 00:12:00.980
very prevalent.

00:12:00.980 --> 00:12:03.880
So roughly one third
of New York City middle

00:12:03.880 --> 00:12:07.810
and high school screen students
on past academic performance

00:12:07.810 --> 00:12:08.790
in 2018.

00:12:08.790 --> 00:12:12.570
So prior to the suspension
of academic screening,

00:12:12.570 --> 00:12:14.820
one third of middle
schools in New York City

00:12:14.820 --> 00:12:17.910
were admitting students based
on their past performance.

00:12:17.910 --> 00:12:20.610
So you would get your
admission at a school

00:12:20.610 --> 00:12:24.920
based on whether you did
well in elementary school.

00:12:24.920 --> 00:12:26.620
So in response to
this challenge,

00:12:26.620 --> 00:12:29.860
in 2019, two New York
City school district--

00:12:29.860 --> 00:12:32.170
so as Josh said, New
York City is very big.

00:12:32.170 --> 00:12:34.690
And so, it's divided in
32 local school districts

00:12:34.690 --> 00:12:36.250
that you can see
in this picture.

00:12:36.250 --> 00:12:37.530
And two of them--

00:12:37.530 --> 00:12:41.050
so the Upper West Side District,
which is in blue up there,

00:12:41.050 --> 00:12:44.080
and the Northwest
Brooklyn District,

00:12:44.080 --> 00:12:47.740
which is orange down here--
so it's in the Northwest

00:12:47.740 --> 00:12:49.540
of Brooklyn--

00:12:49.540 --> 00:12:53.090
decided to adopt diversity
management plans.

00:12:53.090 --> 00:12:55.780
So the idea behind
these plans were

00:12:55.780 --> 00:12:58.540
to try to integrate the
middle school in these two

00:12:58.540 --> 00:13:01.600
districts of the city, which
is as Josh pointed out,

00:13:01.600 --> 00:13:05.170
the wealthiest district in the
city, and also the two district

00:13:05.170 --> 00:13:08.140
in which segregation
was very prevalent.

00:13:08.140 --> 00:13:10.360
And so, this district
decided to reduce

00:13:10.360 --> 00:13:13.150
the role of academic
screens in admissions

00:13:13.150 --> 00:13:16.420
and in order to try to
increase integration

00:13:16.420 --> 00:13:18.490
at the district schools.

00:13:18.490 --> 00:13:22.060
So then, in my research, what
I have to try to understand

00:13:22.060 --> 00:13:24.460
is whether this
reform worked out.

00:13:24.460 --> 00:13:28.720
So whether actually
they achieved their aim

00:13:28.720 --> 00:13:31.720
and what were the
consequences in terms

00:13:31.720 --> 00:13:35.110
of enrollment and application
behavior from [INAUDIBLE]..

00:13:35.110 --> 00:13:38.200
Whether this reform
had other consequences

00:13:38.200 --> 00:13:39.790
on how people choose
the school they

00:13:39.790 --> 00:13:45.220
apply to and which school
they decide to enroll in.

00:13:45.220 --> 00:13:51.670
So I want to present three key
findings from this research.

00:13:51.670 --> 00:13:53.620
Before diving into
the first finding,

00:13:53.620 --> 00:13:56.260
I just wanted to give
you some more background

00:13:56.260 --> 00:13:59.740
information about what exactly
these two district did.

00:13:59.740 --> 00:14:06.130
So these district-- so they
both enacted reform 2019.

00:14:06.130 --> 00:14:08.310
And the reform were
slightly different.

00:14:08.310 --> 00:14:11.080
So in Upper West Side,
what the district did

00:14:11.080 --> 00:14:14.470
was, that they decided to
keep academic screening.

00:14:14.470 --> 00:14:16.210
So students would
still be screened

00:14:16.210 --> 00:14:19.900
based on their past test
scores when they were

00:14:19.900 --> 00:14:21.460
applying for middle school.

00:14:21.460 --> 00:14:25.630
But they set aside 25% of
seats in each middle school

00:14:25.630 --> 00:14:30.070
for students that are eligible
for a reduced price lunch

00:14:30.070 --> 00:14:32.430
and have low test scores.

00:14:32.430 --> 00:14:35.110
So if you were a student
in the Upper West Side,

00:14:35.110 --> 00:14:38.200
and you had a test score
below a certain threshold,

00:14:38.200 --> 00:14:41.440
and you were also eligible for
free or reduced price lunch,

00:14:41.440 --> 00:14:45.400
then you would get a priority
for this 25% of seats.

00:14:45.400 --> 00:14:48.490
So the idea was to expand
the access to the school

00:14:48.490 --> 00:14:52.900
to students who are low
income and low achieving.

00:14:52.900 --> 00:14:55.950
In Northwest Brooklyn, the
reform was a bit more radical.

00:14:55.950 --> 00:14:58.680
So the idea was, the
district decided actually

00:14:58.680 --> 00:15:00.570
to eliminate all
academic screen.

00:15:00.570 --> 00:15:04.760
So what that means, is that
middle school would only

00:15:04.760 --> 00:15:07.620
admit student based on
their lottery numbers.

00:15:07.620 --> 00:15:09.505
So in New York City
every year, students

00:15:09.505 --> 00:15:11.505
are assigned lottery
number, and that determines

00:15:11.505 --> 00:15:13.210
in part their admission.

00:15:13.210 --> 00:15:18.420
And so, in Northwest Brooklyn,
after the reform in 2019,

00:15:18.420 --> 00:15:21.150
students would be admitted
only based on that number

00:15:21.150 --> 00:15:25.110
and not based on their
past academic performance.

00:15:25.110 --> 00:15:28.950
The district also set
aside 52% of seats

00:15:28.950 --> 00:15:31.980
for students who are
low-income English language

00:15:31.980 --> 00:15:33.030
learners or homeless.

00:15:33.030 --> 00:15:38.130
So which means that students
that would qualify for these C

00:15:38.130 --> 00:15:42.930
priority would be
put first on the list

00:15:42.930 --> 00:15:45.390
when the school were
filling their seats.

00:15:45.390 --> 00:15:48.120
That means, they would
get an extra priority

00:15:48.120 --> 00:15:51.570
for this 52% of seats.

00:15:51.570 --> 00:15:53.820
And so, the first thing
I found in my research

00:15:53.820 --> 00:15:56.970
is that this policy that
reduced the role played

00:15:56.970 --> 00:16:00.660
by academic screens decreased
racial inequality in school

00:16:00.660 --> 00:16:02.740
segregation to some extent.

00:16:02.740 --> 00:16:04.690
And so, that's what
I'm showing you here.

00:16:04.690 --> 00:16:08.100
So it is maybe a
complicated graph.

00:16:08.100 --> 00:16:12.600
There, I'm displaying you the
evolution of school segregation

00:16:12.600 --> 00:16:13.980
for Black districts.

00:16:13.980 --> 00:16:17.430
In blue, you can see the
evolution of school segregation

00:16:17.430 --> 00:16:21.150
for Upper West Side, in
orange for Northwest Brooklyn,

00:16:21.150 --> 00:16:24.660
and in gray for other New
York City school districts.

00:16:24.660 --> 00:16:26.940
The left panel is
school segregation

00:16:26.940 --> 00:16:28.620
for low-income students.

00:16:28.620 --> 00:16:32.640
So that correspond to measure
of school economic school

00:16:32.640 --> 00:16:33.870
segregation.

00:16:33.870 --> 00:16:36.120
While the right panel
is school segregation

00:16:36.120 --> 00:16:37.980
for Black and Hispanic students.

00:16:37.980 --> 00:16:42.620
So that corresponds to
measure of racial segregation.

00:16:42.620 --> 00:16:44.990
As you may see,
for the gray line,

00:16:44.990 --> 00:16:46.940
is flat, which
means that there was

00:16:46.940 --> 00:16:49.910
no change in school segregation
in other district in the city.

00:16:49.910 --> 00:16:52.880
Which makes sense, because
there was no change in policy.

00:16:52.880 --> 00:16:55.100
Whereas we can see
a decrease in school

00:16:55.100 --> 00:16:58.190
segregation for
Northwest Brooklyn

00:16:58.190 --> 00:17:01.730
after the enactment
of the reforms.

00:17:01.730 --> 00:17:04.721
So school segregation
were roughly at 15%,

00:17:04.721 --> 00:17:10.040
and it dropped around
10 percentage points

00:17:10.040 --> 00:17:11.690
after the reform.

00:17:11.690 --> 00:17:14.960
For Upper West Side, the
picture is less clear.

00:17:14.960 --> 00:17:16.520
The decrease is less marked.

00:17:16.520 --> 00:17:21.200
And this is consistent with the
fact that the plan they adopted

00:17:21.200 --> 00:17:23.119
was a bit less radical.

00:17:23.119 --> 00:17:25.339
As you can see as well,
the drop in segregation

00:17:25.339 --> 00:17:28.310
is more marked for
low income students

00:17:28.310 --> 00:17:30.630
than for Black and
Hispanic students.

00:17:30.630 --> 00:17:32.840
So that means that
there was a larger

00:17:32.840 --> 00:17:35.180
decrease in economic
school segregation

00:17:35.180 --> 00:17:38.520
than racial specific.

00:17:38.520 --> 00:17:40.620
The second key
finding of my research

00:17:40.620 --> 00:17:44.040
was that, actually, families
reacted to this reform.

00:17:44.040 --> 00:17:46.980
And they reacted by
changing their application

00:17:46.980 --> 00:17:49.470
and enrollment behaviors.

00:17:49.470 --> 00:17:53.240
So what I mean by that is
that, when finally decided

00:17:53.240 --> 00:17:57.510
where to enroll, what I found
is that after the reform,

00:17:57.510 --> 00:17:59.710
some group of students
were more likely to enroll

00:17:59.710 --> 00:18:01.850
in a school outside the
traditional public school

00:18:01.850 --> 00:18:02.872
system.

00:18:02.872 --> 00:18:05.330
So they were more likely not
to enroll in the New York City

00:18:05.330 --> 00:18:07.340
public school but to
enroll either in a charter

00:18:07.340 --> 00:18:08.642
or in a private school.

00:18:08.642 --> 00:18:10.100
And the group of
students that were

00:18:10.100 --> 00:18:12.980
more likely to exit the
public school system

00:18:12.980 --> 00:18:15.830
was white and higher
income students.

00:18:15.830 --> 00:18:19.438
Specifically, they were
6% to 8% more likely

00:18:19.438 --> 00:18:21.980
to enroll in a school that was
outside the traditional public

00:18:21.980 --> 00:18:23.360
school system.

00:18:23.360 --> 00:18:27.170
And to give you an
idea, the baseline rate

00:18:27.170 --> 00:18:30.050
of enrollment outside
the public school system

00:18:30.050 --> 00:18:35.130
is of 10% in Upper West
Side and 17% in [INAUDIBLE]..

00:18:35.130 --> 00:18:38.580
So this increasing
exits is substantial.

00:18:38.580 --> 00:18:40.490
On the other hand,
Black, Hispanic, Asian,

00:18:40.490 --> 00:18:42.240
and lower-income
students were unaffected.

00:18:42.240 --> 00:18:44.198
So they enrolled the same
rate in public school

00:18:44.198 --> 00:18:46.510
after the reform than before.

00:18:46.510 --> 00:18:51.370
Then, I tried to investigate
why white and higher income

00:18:51.370 --> 00:18:53.110
students were more
likely to exit.

00:18:53.110 --> 00:18:56.110
And what I found is
that family seems

00:18:56.110 --> 00:19:00.580
to be responding to the
fact that after the reform,

00:19:00.580 --> 00:19:03.460
children were assigned to school
with lower achieving peers

00:19:03.460 --> 00:19:04.520
on average.

00:19:04.520 --> 00:19:06.520
So it seems to be the
case that families

00:19:06.520 --> 00:19:09.610
are leaving the district
in response to the fact

00:19:09.610 --> 00:19:13.210
that they were assigned to a
school in which their peers--

00:19:13.210 --> 00:19:18.280
the classmate of their kid would
have had lower test scores.

00:19:18.280 --> 00:19:20.020
On the application
side, what I found

00:19:20.020 --> 00:19:23.770
is that there was also
a change in the type

00:19:23.770 --> 00:19:25.690
of school families applied to.

00:19:25.690 --> 00:19:29.860
So after these reforms finally
started applying to more

00:19:29.860 --> 00:19:33.280
schools, and students
that were low-achieving--

00:19:33.280 --> 00:19:36.320
so that had lower grades
in elementary schools--

00:19:36.320 --> 00:19:39.070
decided to apply to more
competitive schools.

00:19:39.070 --> 00:19:41.907
And this application
response tended

00:19:41.907 --> 00:19:43.990
to increase the effectiveness
of the [INAUDIBLE]..

00:19:43.990 --> 00:19:46.510
Because students that
were lower-achieving

00:19:46.510 --> 00:19:48.940
and they're often also
lower-income, ended up

00:19:48.940 --> 00:19:50.950
applying to school
that were enrolling

00:19:50.950 --> 00:19:54.820
more higher-income students.

00:19:54.820 --> 00:19:57.610
Finally, as a last step of my
analysis, what I tried to do

00:19:57.610 --> 00:20:00.310
is to understand what would
happen if the city was

00:20:00.310 --> 00:20:02.660
to enact a similar reform.

00:20:02.660 --> 00:20:07.450
And so, I basically
perform a simulation

00:20:07.450 --> 00:20:10.070
of what would a school
segregation look

00:20:10.070 --> 00:20:12.940
like in New York City if
the city was to change

00:20:12.940 --> 00:20:14.642
its admissions system.

00:20:14.642 --> 00:20:16.600
That's why I'm showing
you in these bar charts.

00:20:16.600 --> 00:20:19.060
In these bar charts, you
have the level of segregation

00:20:19.060 --> 00:20:24.280
in purple and under the
actual admission regime.

00:20:24.280 --> 00:20:27.550
Then in green, I have a
counterfactual admission regime

00:20:27.550 --> 00:20:31.030
in which the city is not using
academic achievement to screen

00:20:31.030 --> 00:20:32.420
students anymore.

00:20:32.420 --> 00:20:36.250
And finally, in red, you have a
regime in which both as a city

00:20:36.250 --> 00:20:40.575
is not using either academic
screens or geographic screens.

00:20:40.575 --> 00:20:41.950
What I mean by
geographic screens

00:20:41.950 --> 00:20:46.090
is screening based on where
does this student live.

00:20:46.090 --> 00:20:49.210
In New York City, you
may get some priority

00:20:49.210 --> 00:20:51.160
because you live
close to a school.

00:20:51.160 --> 00:20:54.610
And so, if the city was also
to drop this kind of priority,

00:20:54.610 --> 00:20:57.483
you would get to the red bar.

00:20:57.483 --> 00:20:58.900
So from this
picture, what you can

00:20:58.900 --> 00:21:00.358
see is that the
drop in segregation

00:21:00.358 --> 00:21:02.890
is much larger when the
city is also dropping

00:21:02.890 --> 00:21:05.860
geographic screens,
which tend to entail

00:21:05.860 --> 00:21:08.030
that geographic screen
are a bigger driver

00:21:08.030 --> 00:21:10.810
of segregation than
academic screens.

00:21:10.810 --> 00:21:13.900
But you can also see that these
still residual segregation

00:21:13.900 --> 00:21:16.360
even when the city is
not admitting students

00:21:16.360 --> 00:21:19.780
based on either academic
or geographic screens.

00:21:19.780 --> 00:21:22.240
And this residual
segregation is clearly

00:21:22.240 --> 00:21:26.630
due to family preferences
and residential segregation.

00:21:26.630 --> 00:21:29.530
So the fact that the city
is segregated by people

00:21:29.530 --> 00:21:32.538
from different race and
different socioeconomic

00:21:32.538 --> 00:21:34.330
background live in
different neighborhoods.

00:21:34.330 --> 00:21:37.300
And they tend to send your
kids to different school

00:21:37.300 --> 00:21:39.440
and also to apply to
different schools.

00:21:39.440 --> 00:21:42.430
So even if the city were
not to use any admission

00:21:42.430 --> 00:21:44.320
criteria to admit
students to the schools,

00:21:44.320 --> 00:21:48.700
you would still have this
remaining segregation.

00:21:48.700 --> 00:21:51.450
So taking stock, I
think that's what

00:21:51.450 --> 00:21:54.660
I learned from this project
is that admission reform has

00:21:54.660 --> 00:21:57.210
the potential for curbing
segregation in New York City

00:21:57.210 --> 00:21:58.350
schools.

00:21:58.350 --> 00:22:01.590
And why I'm saying that
is because roughly 50%

00:22:01.590 --> 00:22:03.330
of school segregation
is accounted

00:22:03.330 --> 00:22:07.450
by academic and geographic
admission screens.

00:22:07.450 --> 00:22:08.950
Geographic screens
appear, actually,

00:22:08.950 --> 00:22:10.920
to be more important
than academic screens.

00:22:10.920 --> 00:22:15.900
So if the city was willing
to lower segregation more,

00:22:15.900 --> 00:22:17.400
according to my
simulation, it would

00:22:17.400 --> 00:22:20.310
be more effective to
drop geographic screens.

00:22:20.310 --> 00:22:22.410
Nonetheless, one have
to take into account

00:22:22.410 --> 00:22:25.800
that it appears that white and
high-income applicants could

00:22:25.800 --> 00:22:28.290
exit the traditional public
school district in response

00:22:28.290 --> 00:22:29.430
to such reform.

00:22:29.430 --> 00:22:31.770
So it's unclear to
what extent they would.

00:22:31.770 --> 00:22:35.430
But it seemed to be the case
that white and high-income

00:22:35.430 --> 00:22:39.210
applicants tend to exit the
public school district when

00:22:39.210 --> 00:22:41.520
there is an admission
reform that [INAUDIBLE]

00:22:41.520 --> 00:22:45.150
importance of screening.

00:22:45.150 --> 00:22:47.880
I think that these
are interesting if you

00:22:47.880 --> 00:22:52.020
put into the context that
New York City is actually

00:22:52.020 --> 00:22:55.030
currently considering
further education reforms.

00:22:55.030 --> 00:22:56.580
So in particular,
as the city has

00:22:56.580 --> 00:22:59.220
been discussing the removal
of geographic screens

00:22:59.220 --> 00:23:01.440
in high schools
and the phase out

00:23:01.440 --> 00:23:04.000
of gifted and talented
programs in elementary schools.

00:23:04.000 --> 00:23:07.530
And so, I mean, in
light of my analysis,

00:23:07.530 --> 00:23:10.320
it would be curious to see
whether these policies are

00:23:10.320 --> 00:23:14.580
implemented and how they
fare if they are implemented.

00:23:14.580 --> 00:23:17.870
And I think that's
all I had to say.

00:23:17.870 --> 00:23:20.440
I'm going to give
it back to Parag.

00:23:20.440 --> 00:23:21.370
Terrific, Clémence.

00:23:21.370 --> 00:23:22.540
Thank you so much.

00:23:22.540 --> 00:23:26.890
That's a really fascinating
and topical work.

00:23:26.890 --> 00:23:31.870
What I want to do now is open
it up to a conversation with you

00:23:31.870 --> 00:23:35.320
and also with Josh before
opening it more generally

00:23:35.320 --> 00:23:38.470
to the broader audience.

00:23:38.470 --> 00:23:41.020
And Clémence, let me start
off just by asking a couple

00:23:41.020 --> 00:23:43.360
of questions about your work.

00:23:46.150 --> 00:23:49.000
And you mentioned New York City
is the nation's largest school

00:23:49.000 --> 00:23:49.540
district.

00:23:49.540 --> 00:23:53.200
And a lot of the country
has been paying attention

00:23:53.200 --> 00:23:56.080
to the efforts to try to
integrate the Upper West

00:23:56.080 --> 00:23:58.720
Side and Northwest Brooklyn.

00:23:58.720 --> 00:24:00.310
What I wanted to
ask you about is

00:24:00.310 --> 00:24:02.950
if you could speak on
what the reaction has

00:24:02.950 --> 00:24:07.390
been from your contacts in
New York City to your results.

00:24:07.390 --> 00:24:09.430
And more generally,
if you could say

00:24:09.430 --> 00:24:11.890
a bit more about
your relationship

00:24:11.890 --> 00:24:14.170
with the folks at the
Department of Education,

00:24:14.170 --> 00:24:16.690
and what it's been
like to do research

00:24:16.690 --> 00:24:21.470
that is so topical and
sometimes emotionally charged.

00:24:21.470 --> 00:24:22.940
Yeah.

00:24:22.940 --> 00:24:27.320
So this research
was mostly possible

00:24:27.320 --> 00:24:30.080
because of the collaboration
of the New York City Office

00:24:30.080 --> 00:24:33.020
of Enrollment and Research.

00:24:33.020 --> 00:24:35.570
And I think it was a
very interesting part

00:24:35.570 --> 00:24:39.620
of the work, actually,
this collaboration.

00:24:39.620 --> 00:24:43.970
Because so, they bring more
a policy-oriented approach

00:24:43.970 --> 00:24:44.570
to the work.

00:24:44.570 --> 00:24:47.960
And they know better what
is playing on the ground.

00:24:47.960 --> 00:24:52.250
What are policymakers' worries,
what families are saying

00:24:52.250 --> 00:24:53.960
in reaction to this policy.

00:24:53.960 --> 00:24:58.010
And so, it was great
very good interaction

00:24:58.010 --> 00:25:00.020
to orient the research
and to understand

00:25:00.020 --> 00:25:02.780
what might be important
and what might be feasible.

00:25:08.620 --> 00:25:14.450
So in general, I think that
these kind of policymaker

00:25:14.450 --> 00:25:18.740
and academic interaction are
very relevant for research.

00:25:18.740 --> 00:25:20.720
And it could give you
a different perspective

00:25:20.720 --> 00:25:24.050
from your work that what you
would have just by looking

00:25:24.050 --> 00:25:26.060
at your result by yourself.

00:25:26.060 --> 00:25:28.820
And it was very enriching for
me to collaborate with the New

00:25:28.820 --> 00:25:32.370
York City School District and
get their feedback on my work.

00:25:32.370 --> 00:25:34.070
And it was very much--

00:25:34.070 --> 00:25:38.060
we had a lot of discussion
when I was developing the work.

00:25:38.060 --> 00:25:40.310
And I could take into
account their reaction

00:25:40.310 --> 00:25:42.330
to my early findings.

00:25:42.330 --> 00:25:43.760
So that was quite interesting.

00:25:46.350 --> 00:25:47.080
That's great.

00:25:47.080 --> 00:25:50.910
It seems like that partnership
has been quite fruitful.

00:25:50.910 --> 00:25:56.310
And let me turn to
another question, which

00:25:56.310 --> 00:25:59.400
is maybe going to ask you to
speculate a little bit more.

00:25:59.400 --> 00:26:03.330
So you mentioned some of the
new reforms that were proposed.

00:26:03.330 --> 00:26:06.630
And we've just had an
election in New York City,

00:26:06.630 --> 00:26:08.400
where there's a new mayor.

00:26:08.400 --> 00:26:11.340
And New York City is a
school district where

00:26:11.340 --> 00:26:13.200
the mayor has a lot of control.

00:26:13.200 --> 00:26:15.150
It doesn't have an
elected school board

00:26:15.150 --> 00:26:17.640
unlike many other cities.

00:26:17.640 --> 00:26:21.990
What are the big issues in New
York City schools these days?

00:26:21.990 --> 00:26:23.850
And if you could
whisper into the ear

00:26:23.850 --> 00:26:26.220
of the mayor and the
new superintendent

00:26:26.220 --> 00:26:30.300
about the coming issues,
what would you tell them

00:26:30.300 --> 00:26:34.290
about what they
should focus on based

00:26:34.290 --> 00:26:36.900
on your research and your
knowledge on this topic?

00:26:40.490 --> 00:26:47.360
That's a great
question. (LAUGHS)

00:26:47.360 --> 00:26:51.950
So I think, there has been a lot
of-- so I think that actually,

00:26:51.950 --> 00:26:55.200
like you mentioned, that the
mayor has a lot of power.

00:26:55.200 --> 00:26:57.930
So there's also a lot of
uncertainty of what's going on.

00:26:57.930 --> 00:27:00.500
And I think that something
I've seen a lot in my work

00:27:00.500 --> 00:27:03.710
is that parental
understanding of the system

00:27:03.710 --> 00:27:07.140
is somewhat heated sometimes.

00:27:07.140 --> 00:27:10.112
So I think that a
very basic thing that

00:27:10.112 --> 00:27:11.570
would be useful in
New York City is

00:27:11.570 --> 00:27:15.770
to have a better
communication between parents

00:27:15.770 --> 00:27:16.790
and the administration.

00:27:16.790 --> 00:27:22.100
So actually, the
reform in district 15

00:27:22.100 --> 00:27:25.100
was a great example of that,
in which the reform went

00:27:25.100 --> 00:27:28.610
relatively smoothly, even
though it was actually

00:27:28.610 --> 00:27:31.310
dropping all academic
screens because there was

00:27:31.310 --> 00:27:33.350
a lot of community involvement.

00:27:33.350 --> 00:27:38.210
And so, that's something
that maybe would be useful.

00:27:38.210 --> 00:27:40.160
Because we've been
talking a lot about all

00:27:40.160 --> 00:27:42.720
the possible admission
reform that could happen.

00:27:42.720 --> 00:27:45.030
And every time, it seems
like there's a backlash.

00:27:45.030 --> 00:27:46.748
And there's not
much understanding

00:27:46.748 --> 00:27:48.040
of what's going to happen next.

00:27:48.040 --> 00:27:51.640
So I think that that
would be an interesting--

00:27:51.640 --> 00:27:53.750
in terms of admission
policies, it

00:27:53.750 --> 00:27:57.530
would be great and
quite important to get

00:27:57.530 --> 00:27:58.760
the communication clear.

00:27:58.760 --> 00:28:00.980
Also because it seems
to be that there's

00:28:00.980 --> 00:28:03.590
some barriers to information
in New York City.

00:28:03.590 --> 00:28:05.810
So there was a study
a couple of years

00:28:05.810 --> 00:28:08.420
ago that showed that students
didn't really understand

00:28:08.420 --> 00:28:09.620
what their options were.

00:28:09.620 --> 00:28:15.080
And so, that could
be an easier step

00:28:15.080 --> 00:28:18.320
to take, than overhauls in the
whole admissions system just

00:28:18.320 --> 00:28:20.900
to reduce barrier
of information.

00:28:23.420 --> 00:28:23.960
Terrific.

00:28:23.960 --> 00:28:25.070
Great.

00:28:25.070 --> 00:28:28.970
Let me see if we can try to
get Josh also to weigh in

00:28:28.970 --> 00:28:31.080
on our next question here.

00:28:31.080 --> 00:28:32.630
And this is a
question a bit more

00:28:32.630 --> 00:28:38.420
about what economists bring to
debates about education policy.

00:28:38.420 --> 00:28:41.090
So much of the
conversation about efforts

00:28:41.090 --> 00:28:43.970
to integrate and
desegregate schools

00:28:43.970 --> 00:28:48.530
are based on strong moral and
ethical arguments, or arguments

00:28:48.530 --> 00:28:51.710
that date at least to the
Brown versus Board of Education

00:28:51.710 --> 00:28:53.750
decision in the 1950s.

00:28:53.750 --> 00:28:55.850
And Brown casts a
very long shadow

00:28:55.850 --> 00:28:59.810
on even debates in
the current era.

00:28:59.810 --> 00:29:01.837
I'm curious, what
does an economist--

00:29:01.837 --> 00:29:03.170
someone who works with numbers--

00:29:03.170 --> 00:29:06.140
really have to say about these
moral and ethical issues?

00:29:06.140 --> 00:29:08.330
How does an economist
approach questions

00:29:08.330 --> 00:29:11.000
about the effects
of segregation?

00:29:11.000 --> 00:29:12.740
Josh, maybe you
want to go first.

00:29:12.740 --> 00:29:15.840
And then, we'll
hand it to Clémence?

00:29:15.840 --> 00:29:16.470
Sure.

00:29:16.470 --> 00:29:18.120
Well, I don't know
if I could speak

00:29:18.120 --> 00:29:20.470
for all economists, of course.

00:29:20.470 --> 00:29:25.470
But speaking for
myself, and I think

00:29:25.470 --> 00:29:30.780
this is the ethos
in the lab, we would

00:29:30.780 --> 00:29:33.210
like to see schools integrated.

00:29:33.210 --> 00:29:39.420
But we approach questions
related to segregation,

00:29:39.420 --> 00:29:43.920
and school assignment,
and school effectiveness

00:29:43.920 --> 00:29:44.910
dispassionately--

00:29:44.910 --> 00:29:47.580
I think that's our
primary contribution.

00:29:47.580 --> 00:29:51.330
We're interested in the
consequences of, for example,

00:29:51.330 --> 00:29:53.160
descreening.

00:29:53.160 --> 00:29:56.040
Descreening is well-intentioned,
but that doesn't

00:29:56.040 --> 00:29:58.620
mean that it works out well.

00:29:58.620 --> 00:30:02.970
We need to consider the
consequences of that.

00:30:02.970 --> 00:30:05.760
Did that actually
increase access?

00:30:05.760 --> 00:30:09.820
And also, we need to
confront possibilities

00:30:09.820 --> 00:30:10.810
like white flight.

00:30:10.810 --> 00:30:15.940
And Clémence's study addresses
these issues beautifully.

00:30:15.940 --> 00:30:20.770
And she approaches it without
any preconceived notions.

00:30:20.770 --> 00:30:26.920
And the facts are there for
policymakers to consider.

00:30:26.920 --> 00:30:30.730
Of course, that's not the only
consideration that's relevant.

00:30:30.730 --> 00:30:32.950
But I think it's
essential to know

00:30:32.950 --> 00:30:36.760
what the actual consequences
of an intervention

00:30:36.760 --> 00:30:40.420
like descreening are.

00:30:40.420 --> 00:30:45.310
And our role is to provide that
kind of objective information.

00:30:45.310 --> 00:30:48.520
And then, it's up
to decision-makers

00:30:48.520 --> 00:30:50.060
how they use it.

00:30:50.060 --> 00:30:52.840
But we can also help
guide that discussion.

00:30:52.840 --> 00:30:57.580
For example, simulations of the
kind that Clémence does can be

00:30:57.580 --> 00:31:02.520
used to explore consequences of
things that have not yet been

00:31:02.520 --> 00:31:03.360
tried.

00:31:03.360 --> 00:31:06.600
So for example,
descreening citywide.

00:31:06.600 --> 00:31:09.150
There our results
are more speculative.

00:31:09.150 --> 00:31:12.360
But again, we approach
these problems and questions

00:31:12.360 --> 00:31:15.630
as scientists, when we
answer the question, what

00:31:15.630 --> 00:31:18.750
are the consequences
of descreening.

00:31:18.750 --> 00:31:21.220
Then there's a separate
follow-on step,

00:31:21.220 --> 00:31:22.965
which is how that
information is used.

00:31:26.180 --> 00:31:27.100
Thank you, Josh.

00:31:27.100 --> 00:31:30.520
Clémence, maybe I could ask
you just to add a couple

00:31:30.520 --> 00:31:33.550
of thoughts on how economists
have looked at the effects

00:31:33.550 --> 00:31:35.230
of segregation--

00:31:35.230 --> 00:31:38.300
either in New York or elsewhere.

00:31:38.300 --> 00:31:41.740
So I think that something
that I haven't touched on

00:31:41.740 --> 00:31:45.580
and that is interesting is
whether that effect also--

00:31:45.580 --> 00:31:47.580
like as Josh points
out, in our lab,

00:31:47.580 --> 00:31:50.350
we have very interesting
effect on test scores,

00:31:50.350 --> 00:31:55.810
and maybe also on other measure
of development of freedom.

00:31:55.810 --> 00:32:01.180
And this is not something that
is very excellent evidence as

00:32:01.180 --> 00:32:01.700
of now.

00:32:01.700 --> 00:32:04.660
So that's something that
would be interesting to see

00:32:04.660 --> 00:32:06.020
more work on in the future.

00:32:06.020 --> 00:32:12.140
So there are some evidence that
when desegregation happened

00:32:12.140 --> 00:32:15.850
in the '70s, and '80s,
that was a '60s, '70s,

00:32:15.850 --> 00:32:18.760
that was beneficial
to Black students that

00:32:18.760 --> 00:32:20.290
had an increase in test scores.

00:32:20.290 --> 00:32:23.110
Nonetheless, it's hard to know
whether it was because increase

00:32:23.110 --> 00:32:26.330
in funding or just the
exposure to more diverse peers.

00:32:26.330 --> 00:32:30.220
So that is something that
clearly an economist would

00:32:30.220 --> 00:32:32.650
be interesting in studying.

00:32:32.650 --> 00:32:36.700
There are barriers, is not
an easy question to tackle.

00:32:36.700 --> 00:32:41.300
And this study would have been
a good one, but [INAUDIBLE]..

00:32:41.300 --> 00:32:43.180
So there were some limits here.

00:32:43.180 --> 00:32:45.340
But clearly, that's
something which economists

00:32:45.340 --> 00:32:48.790
would weigh in, which is
more the dimension of benefit

00:32:48.790 --> 00:32:49.900
from integration.

00:32:49.900 --> 00:32:53.050
And there's no very
striking evidence

00:32:53.050 --> 00:32:55.910
for that in a more recent year.

00:32:55.910 --> 00:33:01.210
So there are some evidence
from historical desegregation,

00:33:01.210 --> 00:33:06.270
in fact, but not from these
more contemporary efforts.

00:33:06.270 --> 00:33:06.770
Terrific.

00:33:06.770 --> 00:33:08.600
Great.

00:33:08.600 --> 00:33:12.920
Now, let's maybe zoom out a
little bit from New York City.

00:33:12.920 --> 00:33:17.180
It is the Big Apple, but there
are other cities, Clémence,

00:33:17.180 --> 00:33:21.080
that you've studied and
Josh has also studied.

00:33:21.080 --> 00:33:23.720
Maybe, Clémence, you could
tell us a little bit about some

00:33:23.720 --> 00:33:26.802
of the research you've done
with Boston Public Schools--

00:33:26.802 --> 00:33:28.760
the questions you've
looked at, the approaches,

00:33:28.760 --> 00:33:31.730
and what you've been finding.

00:33:31.730 --> 00:33:32.420
Yes.

00:33:32.420 --> 00:33:34.430
That's, I feel, related.

00:33:34.430 --> 00:33:38.570
In the sense that so in Boston,
I study exam school admission

00:33:38.570 --> 00:33:40.310
policies.

00:33:40.310 --> 00:33:42.260
And what I was
interested in there is

00:33:42.260 --> 00:33:45.080
was to understand whether
you could actually

00:33:45.080 --> 00:33:48.930
increase test score by changing
the admission policies.

00:33:48.930 --> 00:33:52.490
So I was not so much
focusing on diversity,

00:33:52.490 --> 00:33:55.370
but more trying to
understand could you

00:33:55.370 --> 00:33:59.180
design a system in which you
raise test score in the city

00:33:59.180 --> 00:34:02.840
by just changing who gets
admitted to an exam school.

00:34:02.840 --> 00:34:06.650
And what I found is actually
that contrary to what one would

00:34:06.650 --> 00:34:10.400
believe, it's not like the
characteristic of the student

00:34:10.400 --> 00:34:11.510
that matters much.

00:34:11.510 --> 00:34:14.510
So it's not because the student
is a high-achieving student

00:34:14.510 --> 00:34:17.120
that he would benefit
more from an exam score.

00:34:17.120 --> 00:34:19.850
It's not also,
minority students don't

00:34:19.850 --> 00:34:23.150
appear to be benefiting more
from attending an exam school.

00:34:23.150 --> 00:34:26.360
What seems to matter
more is which school

00:34:26.360 --> 00:34:27.560
is their alternative school.

00:34:27.560 --> 00:34:33.110
So which school would have
been the outside option

00:34:33.110 --> 00:34:35.630
of these students
if they had not been

00:34:35.630 --> 00:34:37.130
admitted to an exam school.

00:34:37.130 --> 00:34:40.520
And so, I found that actually
if you were to design system,

00:34:40.520 --> 00:34:42.230
you should give
priority to students

00:34:42.230 --> 00:34:45.199
that live in neighborhood
in which the neighborhood

00:34:45.199 --> 00:34:49.190
school is of worse quality,
you get a higher test score

00:34:49.190 --> 00:34:50.540
for the city.

00:34:50.540 --> 00:34:54.320
What is interesting is that a
neighborhood in which schools

00:34:54.320 --> 00:34:55.760
are not particularly
good are not

00:34:55.760 --> 00:34:57.260
necessarily
neighborhood in which

00:34:57.260 --> 00:35:01.710
a lot of low-income and minority
students live in Boston.

00:35:01.710 --> 00:35:04.530
So this is not necessarily
related to the test

00:35:04.530 --> 00:35:06.080
score of the student either.

00:35:06.080 --> 00:35:08.060
So you need to be--

00:35:08.060 --> 00:35:12.470
to try to actually measure
what the school is providing

00:35:12.470 --> 00:35:15.140
to the kid, and not
just look at what

00:35:15.140 --> 00:35:16.710
are the characteristics
of the kids.

00:35:16.710 --> 00:35:18.210
If you want to
design such a system,

00:35:18.210 --> 00:35:20.780
you need to actually
understand what

00:35:20.780 --> 00:35:22.430
is the value add of
the school, which

00:35:22.430 --> 00:35:26.000
is something us economists
care a lot about, which

00:35:26.000 --> 00:35:29.630
is what the school add to a
[INAUDIBLE] test score, and not

00:35:29.630 --> 00:35:32.635
only what is the kid test
score at the end of the school.

00:35:32.635 --> 00:35:34.010
Because otherwise,
you might just

00:35:34.010 --> 00:35:37.518
be selecting those kids that
would have had very high test

00:35:37.518 --> 00:35:38.060
score anyway.

00:35:38.060 --> 00:35:41.000
So what I find in this
other line of research

00:35:41.000 --> 00:35:45.110
is that you could actually try
to design a system in which you

00:35:45.110 --> 00:35:48.500
target students that have
low value-added alternative.

00:35:48.500 --> 00:35:49.590
And that's more efficient.

00:35:49.590 --> 00:35:51.715
So that's a different
perspective on admission

00:35:51.715 --> 00:35:54.020
that is not so much
focused on diversity,

00:35:54.020 --> 00:35:59.210
but more on how school systems
can raise test score by just

00:35:59.210 --> 00:36:03.620
changing very more
administrative rules

00:36:03.620 --> 00:36:05.990
of admission.

00:36:05.990 --> 00:36:06.500
Terrific.

00:36:06.500 --> 00:36:10.070
And Clémence, that
work in Boston--

00:36:10.070 --> 00:36:12.470
Boston has actually made
some serious changes,

00:36:12.470 --> 00:36:14.780
you mentioned in your
slides-- to their admissions

00:36:14.780 --> 00:36:21.410
policy for these exam schools,
taking into account aspects

00:36:21.410 --> 00:36:22.870
of where kids live.

00:36:22.870 --> 00:36:23.990
Is that right?

00:36:23.990 --> 00:36:25.755
Is that on the table
still in the city?

00:36:25.755 --> 00:36:26.255
Yes.

00:36:26.255 --> 00:36:28.580
Terrific.

00:36:28.580 --> 00:36:30.770
Josh, maybe I'll turn to you.

00:36:30.770 --> 00:36:36.470
So the lab that you co-direct
works in New York and Boston.

00:36:36.470 --> 00:36:40.010
Could you tell us a bit
more about education topics

00:36:40.010 --> 00:36:44.120
that folks at the lab have
addressed-- your own work maybe

00:36:44.120 --> 00:36:47.450
in other cities or
in other settings?

00:36:47.450 --> 00:36:48.620
Sure.

00:36:48.620 --> 00:36:53.450
So the lab that we
share broadly covers

00:36:53.450 --> 00:36:56.330
two sorts of education topics.

00:36:56.330 --> 00:36:58.790
We have our K
through 12 division.

00:36:58.790 --> 00:37:03.500
And that looks at
public education

00:37:03.500 --> 00:37:08.390
in large urban districts like
Boston, New York, Chicago,

00:37:08.390 --> 00:37:11.270
New Orleans, Washington.

00:37:11.270 --> 00:37:14.840
And cities especially
of interest to us

00:37:14.840 --> 00:37:18.140
have some kind of
centralized assignment.

00:37:18.140 --> 00:37:21.710
And that's something also
that the lab specializes

00:37:21.710 --> 00:37:27.170
in studying-- how kids are
offered seats in districts

00:37:27.170 --> 00:37:28.760
that have a lot of choice.

00:37:28.760 --> 00:37:32.720
New York is really
the most interesting,

00:37:32.720 --> 00:37:35.900
because first of all,
it's such a huge place,

00:37:35.900 --> 00:37:37.700
and it has so many schools.

00:37:37.700 --> 00:37:41.010
And they have a very
interesting choice system,

00:37:41.010 --> 00:37:44.960
which mixes some element of
random assignment, actually.

00:37:44.960 --> 00:37:46.820
Lotteries are involved in that.

00:37:46.820 --> 00:37:50.270
But not only-- you also have
the screen schools that Clémence

00:37:50.270 --> 00:37:51.140
talked about.

00:37:51.140 --> 00:37:54.230
And you have the legendary
specialized high schools,

00:37:54.230 --> 00:37:57.320
like Stuyvesant, Brooklyn
Tech, and Bronx Science.

00:37:57.320 --> 00:37:59.210
We're very interested
in who gets

00:37:59.210 --> 00:38:02.240
to go to these different schools
and what the consequences

00:38:02.240 --> 00:38:03.770
of going to them are.

00:38:03.770 --> 00:38:08.210
We also study a lot of the
most exciting and potentially

00:38:08.210 --> 00:38:12.230
controversial education
policy reform projects.

00:38:12.230 --> 00:38:16.310
We've looked extensively
for the past decade

00:38:16.310 --> 00:38:19.250
at different sorts of charter
schools in different districts.

00:38:19.250 --> 00:38:22.020
And we have a lot of
interesting results there.

00:38:22.020 --> 00:38:26.690
I think one of our most exciting
set of findings in that domain

00:38:26.690 --> 00:38:29.540
is the results for Chicago.

00:38:29.540 --> 00:38:33.770
Where actually,
kids in Chicago--

00:38:33.770 --> 00:38:38.150
so by way of background,
Chicago has a large exam school

00:38:38.150 --> 00:38:39.590
sector--

00:38:39.590 --> 00:38:42.680
schools that are competitive
and highly selective,

00:38:42.680 --> 00:38:44.750
like Payton and North Side.

00:38:44.750 --> 00:38:48.470
And what's kind of surprising
about those schools

00:38:48.470 --> 00:38:51.230
is that they actually
seem to reduce

00:38:51.230 --> 00:38:53.210
the achievement of
their applicants

00:38:53.210 --> 00:38:56.810
when the applicant is offered
a seat in that sector.

00:38:56.810 --> 00:38:59.780
And we puzzled over that.

00:38:59.780 --> 00:39:03.710
Maybe exam schools
are not great.

00:39:03.710 --> 00:39:06.800
Maybe there's a lot of selection
bias in terms of who actually

00:39:06.800 --> 00:39:08.240
gets to go to the exam schools.

00:39:08.240 --> 00:39:10.790
But why should they
reduce achievement?

00:39:10.790 --> 00:39:15.320
And that turns out to have a
simple but maybe surprising

00:39:15.320 --> 00:39:16.250
explanation.

00:39:16.250 --> 00:39:19.370
A lot of the kids who apply
to Chicago's exam schools

00:39:19.370 --> 00:39:22.190
have also applied to a
very successful charter

00:39:22.190 --> 00:39:26.090
network called the Noble
Network of charter high schools.

00:39:26.090 --> 00:39:30.770
And it's more impressive
to get into an exam school,

00:39:30.770 --> 00:39:32.270
because that's selective.

00:39:32.270 --> 00:39:34.340
Whereas Noble admits by lottery.

00:39:34.340 --> 00:39:37.100
So kids who apply to
both Noble and charter--

00:39:37.100 --> 00:39:40.430
tend to apply to
both Noble and exam,

00:39:40.430 --> 00:39:42.887
tend to take an exam
offer when they get it.

00:39:42.887 --> 00:39:44.720
And that means that the
likelihood that they

00:39:44.720 --> 00:39:46.430
go to a charter is reduced.

00:39:46.430 --> 00:39:48.560
And those charters are
very high performing,

00:39:48.560 --> 00:39:50.330
and the kids would
have actually learned

00:39:50.330 --> 00:39:52.580
more and been more
likely to go to college

00:39:52.580 --> 00:39:54.260
if they had gone to
the charter school.

00:39:54.260 --> 00:40:00.830
So that's a striking finding
and sure to be controversial

00:40:00.830 --> 00:40:02.030
when we publish it.

00:40:02.030 --> 00:40:06.620
The other dimension
of education reform

00:40:06.620 --> 00:40:09.620
where we have some potentially
controversial findings

00:40:09.620 --> 00:40:11.600
is research on vouchers.

00:40:11.600 --> 00:40:13.130
Of course, Parag
knows this well,

00:40:13.130 --> 00:40:16.790
because he's the leader
of the voucher team.

00:40:16.790 --> 00:40:20.630
Parag and his coauthors have
studied a very interesting

00:40:20.630 --> 00:40:23.450
voucher program in
Louisiana, where

00:40:23.450 --> 00:40:28.760
kids who attend public schools
that are deemed by the state

00:40:28.760 --> 00:40:32.180
to be low-performing
may get vouchers.

00:40:32.180 --> 00:40:33.320
It's not guaranteed.

00:40:33.320 --> 00:40:35.840
There's an element of
random assignment there too.

00:40:35.840 --> 00:40:38.060
And we take advantage
of the random assignment

00:40:38.060 --> 00:40:41.570
of the Louisiana voucher program
to estimate causal effects.

00:40:41.570 --> 00:40:47.660
And it turns out, good hopes
and expectations for vouchers

00:40:47.660 --> 00:40:50.630
notwithstanding, voucher
winners in Louisiana

00:40:50.630 --> 00:40:54.410
tend to come out of that
program with lower test scores.

00:40:54.410 --> 00:40:57.650
And I don't remember if you guys
looked at college in that study

00:40:57.650 --> 00:40:58.805
as well-- just test scores?

00:40:58.805 --> 00:41:00.260
Yeah, it's a short-run study.

00:41:00.260 --> 00:41:01.940
Younger kids, and
it's a short-run.

00:41:01.940 --> 00:41:04.070
But that is also
quite surprising.

00:41:04.070 --> 00:41:06.560
Because the parents who
are applying for those

00:41:06.560 --> 00:41:09.910
vouchers imagine that they're
getting a real opportunity

00:41:09.910 --> 00:41:10.410
there.

00:41:10.410 --> 00:41:12.650
But then, actually,
the causal effect

00:41:12.650 --> 00:41:15.990
of going to a voucher school in
Louisiana seems to be negative.

00:41:15.990 --> 00:41:19.850
So another reason I want to
tell these stories together--

00:41:19.850 --> 00:41:22.040
the Chicago exam school
and the vouchers is--

00:41:22.040 --> 00:41:25.140
I think it highlights
something I mentioned earlier.

00:41:25.140 --> 00:41:28.010
Which is, in the lab, we don't
approach any of these questions

00:41:28.010 --> 00:41:30.710
with any sort of
predisposition to find

00:41:30.710 --> 00:41:32.360
that something is good or bad.

00:41:32.360 --> 00:41:34.490
We're open to what the data say.

00:41:34.490 --> 00:41:38.150
We're not pro-charter,
and we're not pro-exam,

00:41:38.150 --> 00:41:39.680
and we're not pro-voucher.

00:41:39.680 --> 00:41:42.020
We're not anti-voucher,
we're not anti-charter,

00:41:42.020 --> 00:41:43.550
we're just studying
those things.

00:41:43.550 --> 00:41:45.950
And we have a lot of
interesting findings.

00:41:45.950 --> 00:41:50.300
And the results don't align
with any particular ideological

00:41:50.300 --> 00:41:52.190
predispositions, I think.

00:41:52.190 --> 00:41:54.590
I'll just briefly
mentioned that another part

00:41:54.590 --> 00:41:58.790
of the work that the lab does
is related to higher education.

00:41:58.790 --> 00:42:02.540
And we have a very large
ambitious and ongoing

00:42:02.540 --> 00:42:05.870
randomized evaluation
of financial aid

00:42:05.870 --> 00:42:08.010
in the post-secondary sector.

00:42:08.010 --> 00:42:13.520
So these are kids in
Nebraska, who are applying

00:42:13.520 --> 00:42:16.280
to Nebraska's public schools--

00:42:16.280 --> 00:42:18.530
public universities
and colleges.

00:42:18.530 --> 00:42:22.040
And there's a foundation there--

00:42:22.040 --> 00:42:23.720
many will have heard the name--

00:42:23.720 --> 00:42:28.070
the Buffett Foundation--
that pays for about 10%

00:42:28.070 --> 00:42:32.180
of Nebraska's public college and
university students' tuition.

00:42:32.180 --> 00:42:35.180
And we worked-- and
we are still working--

00:42:35.180 --> 00:42:41.360
with the Buffett foundation in
a study that randomly allocates

00:42:41.360 --> 00:42:44.840
some vouchers to some
kids so that we can find--

00:42:44.840 --> 00:42:49.400
not vouchers, sorry-- some
post-secondary awards.

00:42:49.400 --> 00:42:53.030
They're sort of like
vouchers, but it's just cash.

00:42:53.030 --> 00:42:55.460
And but it can only be
used for public colleges

00:42:55.460 --> 00:42:56.510
and universities.

00:42:56.510 --> 00:43:00.120
And we're studying the
effects of higher education

00:43:00.120 --> 00:43:02.330
financial aid on
educational attainment.

00:43:02.330 --> 00:43:05.900
Do the kids who get that
sort of very generous--

00:43:05.900 --> 00:43:08.060
and it is exceptionally
generous aid

00:43:08.060 --> 00:43:10.190
that the Buffett
Foundation pays for.

00:43:10.190 --> 00:43:14.330
They provide every dollar
of college-related costs.

00:43:14.330 --> 00:43:17.090
There's an official number for
that the federal government

00:43:17.090 --> 00:43:17.810
computes.

00:43:17.810 --> 00:43:20.090
So they pay tuition,
they pay room and board,

00:43:20.090 --> 00:43:23.445
they pay books, and
fees, and so on.

00:43:23.445 --> 00:43:25.070
But there's still a
question as to what

00:43:25.070 --> 00:43:26.780
the actual impact
of that money is,

00:43:26.780 --> 00:43:28.250
because a lot of
the beneficiaries

00:43:28.250 --> 00:43:31.250
might have been likely to
complete college anyway--

00:43:31.250 --> 00:43:33.592
the money may not be
consequential for them.

00:43:33.592 --> 00:43:35.300
So that's the kind of
question that we're

00:43:35.300 --> 00:43:36.620
answering in that study.

00:43:36.620 --> 00:43:39.080
And we have very
interesting findings there

00:43:39.080 --> 00:43:42.470
which show that
overall, there is

00:43:42.470 --> 00:43:45.770
an impact of financial aid
on post-secondary attainment.

00:43:45.770 --> 00:43:50.360
But there are many kids who
are going to get these anyway,

00:43:50.360 --> 00:43:55.250
and then there are groups where
the aid is super high impact.

00:43:55.250 --> 00:43:58.730
And our findings suggest
that a more targeted program

00:43:58.730 --> 00:44:01.160
would be likely to be much
more effective in terms

00:44:01.160 --> 00:44:04.240
of increasing
post-secondary attainment.

00:44:04.240 --> 00:44:04.960
Thank you, Josh.

00:44:04.960 --> 00:44:05.920
And thanks, Clémence.

00:44:05.920 --> 00:44:07.825
It's a common theme
of these studies

00:44:07.825 --> 00:44:11.740
that designing more
effective policies

00:44:11.740 --> 00:44:14.650
require studying the
consequences of existing

00:44:14.650 --> 00:44:15.670
public policies.

00:44:15.670 --> 00:44:19.150
And there is hope and
optimism that we can actually

00:44:19.150 --> 00:44:22.550
make resources be
allocated more efficiently.

00:44:22.550 --> 00:44:24.070
Let me turn now
to some questions

00:44:24.070 --> 00:44:27.820
that we're getting from the
folks who've logged on today.

00:44:27.820 --> 00:44:29.680
And there are a
couple of questions

00:44:29.680 --> 00:44:30.690
that are quite related.

00:44:30.690 --> 00:44:32.860
So let me try to combine them.

00:44:32.860 --> 00:44:36.010
Kenneth Wise-- and Clémence,
maybe I'll come to you first

00:44:36.010 --> 00:44:36.670
on this--

00:44:36.670 --> 00:44:40.700
Kenneth Wise is asking
a question about,

00:44:40.700 --> 00:44:45.190
is there a lower bound on
the degree of segregation,

00:44:45.190 --> 00:44:46.630
say, in New York City.

00:44:46.630 --> 00:44:48.730
And I think that's related
to another question

00:44:48.730 --> 00:44:52.840
here about the role
of residential sorting

00:44:52.840 --> 00:44:58.060
patterns in driving school
segregation patterns.

00:44:58.060 --> 00:45:00.940
So a related question
is asking about

00:45:00.940 --> 00:45:05.350
whether systems that have better
public transportation or better

00:45:05.350 --> 00:45:11.410
busing would go further
to try to integrate cities

00:45:11.410 --> 00:45:12.545
like New York.

00:45:12.545 --> 00:45:14.920
So Clémence, I know you've
looked into that a little bit.

00:45:14.920 --> 00:45:16.830
So maybe you could tell us.

00:45:16.830 --> 00:45:20.330
Yeah, that's a great point.

00:45:20.330 --> 00:45:25.720
And so, what I did in my study
is that I further decomposed

00:45:25.720 --> 00:45:26.930
the figure I showed you.

00:45:26.930 --> 00:45:29.200
So I looked at whether
I could explain

00:45:29.200 --> 00:45:31.560
the residual segregation
in the system.

00:45:31.560 --> 00:45:33.970
In particular, I
wanted to understand

00:45:33.970 --> 00:45:36.310
what share of this
residual segregation

00:45:36.310 --> 00:45:39.955
was due to just families
liking different schools,

00:45:39.955 --> 00:45:42.700
and what share was
due to families

00:45:42.700 --> 00:45:44.735
living in different places.

00:45:44.735 --> 00:45:46.360
They all like the
same kind of schools,

00:45:46.360 --> 00:45:48.950
but some people happen
to be much farther.

00:45:48.950 --> 00:45:51.680
And so, they decided not
to send their kids there.

00:45:51.680 --> 00:45:54.890
And what I find is, of the
residual segregation, 50%

00:45:54.890 --> 00:45:57.430
is driven just by
residential sorting.

00:45:57.430 --> 00:46:01.270
So which means, that
even if we imagine

00:46:01.270 --> 00:46:04.790
a world in which everybody
likes the same school,

00:46:04.790 --> 00:46:07.330
there will still be one
quarter of the segregation

00:46:07.330 --> 00:46:09.970
we see today in New York
City just due to the fact

00:46:09.970 --> 00:46:15.040
that people don't want to
commute hours to go to school.

00:46:15.040 --> 00:46:19.000
So probably in these settings,
if public transportation

00:46:19.000 --> 00:46:21.100
was better, you
could probably reduce

00:46:21.100 --> 00:46:26.410
this residual segregation due
to residential segregation

00:46:26.410 --> 00:46:27.100
even more.

00:46:27.100 --> 00:46:30.460
But as of today, with the
current level of transportation

00:46:30.460 --> 00:46:33.990
roughly a quarter of
the total segregation

00:46:33.990 --> 00:46:36.520
is just due to the pattern
of residential sorting

00:46:36.520 --> 00:46:37.960
in the city.

00:46:37.960 --> 00:46:39.730
New York City--
a point on that--

00:46:39.730 --> 00:46:43.000
New York City is quite peculiar,
because residential segregation

00:46:43.000 --> 00:46:44.370
in New York City is strong.

00:46:44.370 --> 00:46:48.080
But it's at a small
geographical scale.

00:46:48.080 --> 00:46:51.280
Which means that in your
immediate surroundings,

00:46:51.280 --> 00:46:54.410
people tend to be of your
same socioeconomic background

00:46:54.410 --> 00:46:55.150
and race.

00:46:55.150 --> 00:46:58.090
But if you go a mile
away already you

00:46:58.090 --> 00:47:00.200
are going to another community.

00:47:00.200 --> 00:47:03.310
So that makes it a city that is
relatively easier to integrate

00:47:03.310 --> 00:47:05.305
than a city like
Chicago, for instance,

00:47:05.305 --> 00:47:09.380
which is segregation in a much
larger scale than in New York

00:47:09.380 --> 00:47:09.880
City.

00:47:12.890 --> 00:47:14.060
Thank you, Clémence.

00:47:14.060 --> 00:47:17.600
There's another question
also about your work

00:47:17.600 --> 00:47:19.100
that I want to turn to next.

00:47:19.100 --> 00:47:25.070
So Bob Mu has asked a
question about information

00:47:25.070 --> 00:47:28.760
and involvement of
parents in these changes

00:47:28.760 --> 00:47:30.530
to enrollment policies.

00:47:30.530 --> 00:47:34.940
So New York City in
the Upper West Side

00:47:34.940 --> 00:47:38.330
and in Northwest Brooklyn,
both of those areas

00:47:38.330 --> 00:47:39.740
used an income quota.

00:47:39.740 --> 00:47:41.660
And they had to set--

00:47:41.660 --> 00:47:47.060
you said 25% in the Upper
West Side and more than 50%

00:47:47.060 --> 00:47:49.010
in Northwest Brooklyn.

00:47:49.010 --> 00:47:50.840
How did they arrive
at those numbers?

00:47:50.840 --> 00:47:54.590
How did they communicate
those numbers to the public?

00:47:54.590 --> 00:47:57.770
And is income the
right thing to target

00:47:57.770 --> 00:48:00.230
in designing these policies,
or should something else

00:48:00.230 --> 00:48:02.600
like your fallback option--

00:48:02.600 --> 00:48:03.890
as you mentioned earlier--

00:48:03.890 --> 00:48:09.420
or your race be a better proxy?

00:48:09.420 --> 00:48:14.420
So for the first
part of the question,

00:48:14.420 --> 00:48:18.330
I know I have more information
for the Brooklyn process.

00:48:18.330 --> 00:48:21.870
But for in their case, the
process lasted two years.

00:48:21.870 --> 00:48:24.150
And there was
several reports that

00:48:24.150 --> 00:48:27.480
were made before they arrive
at the current policy.

00:48:27.480 --> 00:48:31.270
There were a lot of group
work at the local level.

00:48:31.270 --> 00:48:34.710
So that's how they decided
on the actual policy

00:48:34.710 --> 00:48:36.135
they implemented.

00:48:36.135 --> 00:48:38.850
They decided for
52%, because that's

00:48:38.850 --> 00:48:42.960
the share of the free and
reduced price lunch students

00:48:42.960 --> 00:48:44.040
in the district.

00:48:44.040 --> 00:48:46.290
So this way, it's this
kind of arbitrary number

00:48:46.290 --> 00:48:50.400
that is coming from
somewhere, like [INAUDIBLE]..

00:48:50.400 --> 00:48:54.340
For Upper West Side, I
have less information.

00:48:54.340 --> 00:48:57.150
I know that they also
had community meetings.

00:48:57.150 --> 00:49:02.070
And so, there was discussion
with the stakeholders for sure.

00:49:02.070 --> 00:49:05.310
For district 15, it
took a couple of years

00:49:05.310 --> 00:49:06.370
to get to the policy.

00:49:06.370 --> 00:49:08.560
So it was not an
immediate thing.

00:49:08.560 --> 00:49:13.470
And the second question,
should the city

00:49:13.470 --> 00:49:16.390
be targeting something else.

00:49:16.390 --> 00:49:18.450
So it's not like--

00:49:18.450 --> 00:49:21.510
you can't target by
race, because it's

00:49:21.510 --> 00:49:25.710
forbidden by a Supreme
Court ruling from 2007.

00:49:25.710 --> 00:49:30.030
So clearly, some cities
have decided to use proxies,

00:49:30.030 --> 00:49:33.000
like income is relatively
good proxy for that.

00:49:33.000 --> 00:49:37.080
But it's not something you
can legally do in any case.

00:49:37.080 --> 00:49:39.162
Should you target
on other things.

00:49:39.162 --> 00:49:41.620
I think it really depends on
what you're trying to achieve.

00:49:41.620 --> 00:49:44.820
So if you're trying to achieve
a decrease in segregation,

00:49:44.820 --> 00:49:50.443
probably income is
probably a good proxy.

00:49:50.443 --> 00:49:52.110
If you're trying to
increase test score,

00:49:52.110 --> 00:49:53.910
then you might be
interested in more

00:49:53.910 --> 00:49:56.130
in looking what are
the option of students.

00:49:56.130 --> 00:49:58.830
It is not always true that
lower-income students have

00:49:58.830 --> 00:50:00.540
necessarily the worst options.

00:50:00.540 --> 00:50:01.570
It can be the case.

00:50:01.570 --> 00:50:03.520
But it's not
necessarily the case.

00:50:03.520 --> 00:50:05.340
And so, that's where
we're going back

00:50:05.340 --> 00:50:10.650
to the theme of trying to really
take the data as it is, and you

00:50:10.650 --> 00:50:12.660
try to understand
New York City, which

00:50:12.660 --> 00:50:14.820
are the option for
each kid, and how

00:50:14.820 --> 00:50:19.350
you would design your policy in
the basis of this observation.

00:50:19.350 --> 00:50:22.020
I know for Boston, that is
not the case, necessarily,

00:50:22.020 --> 00:50:24.720
that the more income the
worst school of the city.

00:50:24.720 --> 00:50:26.610
But it might be the
case in New York City.

00:50:26.610 --> 00:50:28.440
I haven't really looked at that.

00:50:28.440 --> 00:50:31.090
But that's an open
empirical question.

00:50:31.090 --> 00:50:33.350
And in any case, it depends
on what are your aims.

00:50:33.350 --> 00:50:34.717
What are you trying to achieve.

00:50:34.717 --> 00:50:36.300
Are you trying to
achieve integration?

00:50:36.300 --> 00:50:39.240
Are trying to achieve
higher test scores?

00:50:39.240 --> 00:50:42.870
What is your aim
as a policymaker.

00:50:42.870 --> 00:50:44.460
Terrific, Clémence.

00:50:44.460 --> 00:50:48.280
We're getting close to near
the end of our time together.

00:50:48.280 --> 00:50:50.650
So I want to wrap up
with two questions--

00:50:50.650 --> 00:50:53.760
one for Clémence first
and then one for Josh.

00:50:53.760 --> 00:50:57.210
So Clémence, I know all of us
in the Economics Department

00:50:57.210 --> 00:51:00.360
and Blueprint Labs were so
thrilled when Josh was awarded

00:51:00.360 --> 00:51:03.310
the Nobel Prize this year.

00:51:03.310 --> 00:51:06.720
And I wonder if you could just
offer some comments on what

00:51:06.720 --> 00:51:09.270
it's like working
with Josh, and what

00:51:09.270 --> 00:51:13.890
it's like when your PhD
advisor wins a Nobel Prize.

00:51:13.890 --> 00:51:14.890
Maybe I should sign off.

00:51:14.890 --> 00:51:17.645
[LAUGHTER]

00:51:17.645 --> 00:51:18.145
No, no.

00:51:18.145 --> 00:51:19.600
I have a good story, actually.

00:51:19.600 --> 00:51:22.270
So when I joined
MIT, we are assigned

00:51:22.270 --> 00:51:25.780
to talk to people when
we go and visit this.

00:51:25.780 --> 00:51:29.530
And actually, I had read Josh
books on [INAUDIBLE] in France.

00:51:29.530 --> 00:51:31.867
And he was kind of God for me.

00:51:31.867 --> 00:51:34.450
And so, I was like, I don't want
to meet with him, because I'm

00:51:34.450 --> 00:51:36.920
way too scared to look stupid.

00:51:36.920 --> 00:51:38.770
And I think that
something I've learned

00:51:38.770 --> 00:51:40.780
a lot during these years,
is that you can't look

00:51:40.780 --> 00:51:42.430
too stupid in front of Josh.

00:51:42.430 --> 00:51:45.700
It's OK to say stupid things.

00:51:45.700 --> 00:51:47.530
And so, I think it's
something I really

00:51:47.530 --> 00:51:50.740
enjoyed working with Josh
is that I learned a lot.

00:51:50.740 --> 00:51:55.150
And I felt like it was OK
not to be always right.

00:51:55.150 --> 00:51:57.310
And actually, that's
how you learn.

00:51:57.310 --> 00:51:58.780
Really, you can't
be always right.

00:51:58.780 --> 00:52:01.600
And I find that's a
very enriching part

00:52:01.600 --> 00:52:05.650
of this opportunity
of working with Josh

00:52:05.650 --> 00:52:07.750
is to learn from the best.

00:52:07.750 --> 00:52:11.590
And even have these kind of very
collegial spirit, in which you

00:52:11.590 --> 00:52:14.230
are not like-- you don't
fear saying something stupid

00:52:14.230 --> 00:52:15.110
anymore.

00:52:15.110 --> 00:52:19.046
So I'm very grateful
of the opportunity.

00:52:19.046 --> 00:52:20.171
That's very nice, Clémence.

00:52:20.171 --> 00:52:20.671
Yeah.

00:52:20.671 --> 00:52:22.600
There's an old saying,
if we weren't confused,

00:52:22.600 --> 00:52:24.183
it wouldn't be called
research, right?

00:52:24.183 --> 00:52:24.910
[LAUGHTER]

00:52:24.910 --> 00:52:26.080
Exactly.

00:52:26.080 --> 00:52:27.680
No stupid question.

00:52:27.680 --> 00:52:28.360
[LAUGHTER]

00:52:28.360 --> 00:52:31.940
Josh, let me have you answer
the last question today.

00:52:31.940 --> 00:52:37.090
So I know you're about to
actually receive the Nobel

00:52:37.090 --> 00:52:38.890
Prize in a couple of days.

00:52:38.890 --> 00:52:41.440
And unfortunately, you're
not able to go to Stockholm

00:52:41.440 --> 00:52:44.140
because of COVID concerns.

00:52:44.140 --> 00:52:47.080
But maybe sometime
in the near future.

00:52:47.080 --> 00:52:51.370
When you look back and
reflect on all the excitement

00:52:51.370 --> 00:52:54.400
of the past year, I wonder if
you had any parting thoughts

00:52:54.400 --> 00:52:58.270
for the next generation--
people like Clémence and other

00:52:58.270 --> 00:53:00.550
scholars that are burgeoning--
starting their research

00:53:00.550 --> 00:53:07.570
careers-- on how to proceed
with your research life and any

00:53:07.570 --> 00:53:11.250
other tips that you
want to pass on.

00:53:11.250 --> 00:53:11.950
Sure, Parag.

00:53:11.950 --> 00:53:12.450
Yeah.

00:53:12.450 --> 00:53:15.820
We're getting our medals
in Washington on Monday.

00:53:15.820 --> 00:53:18.810
Unfortunately, can't go to
Stockholm and meet the King.

00:53:18.810 --> 00:53:22.290
But hopefully, the King
will wait for us next year.

00:53:22.290 --> 00:53:29.410
But in some ways, the Nobel
is kind of a distraction.

00:53:29.410 --> 00:53:30.760
I love my work.

00:53:30.760 --> 00:53:33.030
And I hope to be
able to get back

00:53:33.030 --> 00:53:38.040
to it after all this ceremonial
stuff is taken care of.

00:53:38.040 --> 00:53:40.350
Of course, I'm
flattered and honored.

00:53:40.350 --> 00:53:44.140
But I'm lucky to have
a job that I love.

00:53:44.140 --> 00:53:48.870
And I think that's
what our students are

00:53:48.870 --> 00:53:53.070
headed for-- our
graduate students there.

00:53:53.070 --> 00:53:56.190
I hope they'll join me
in loving their work.

00:53:56.190 --> 00:54:00.690
And it's not that every
minute is a good time.

00:54:00.690 --> 00:54:05.370
But we have a lot of
intellectual challenge.

00:54:05.370 --> 00:54:09.180
And one of the wonderful
things about our lab is also,

00:54:09.180 --> 00:54:15.960
it's not just thinking about
things and proving theorems.

00:54:15.960 --> 00:54:19.980
We're very engaged
in the real world.

00:54:19.980 --> 00:54:24.720
We interact with lots of school
districts, and educators,

00:54:24.720 --> 00:54:28.620
and people who are involved
in education policy.

00:54:28.620 --> 00:54:29.850
And that's very enriching.

00:54:29.850 --> 00:54:33.300
And it helps us keep our eye
on the ball-- that there's

00:54:33.300 --> 00:54:35.910
actual children out
there in school,

00:54:35.910 --> 00:54:38.700
and that educational
policy affects

00:54:38.700 --> 00:54:40.140
their lives and their futures.

00:54:40.140 --> 00:54:42.870
So we're just very lucky.

00:54:42.870 --> 00:54:45.240
We get to do something
that really affects

00:54:45.240 --> 00:54:47.250
a lot of people's lives.

00:54:47.250 --> 00:54:50.380
And it's super interesting
and challenging work.

00:54:50.380 --> 00:54:53.910
I can't think of a better job.

00:54:53.910 --> 00:54:54.570
Great, Josh.

00:54:54.570 --> 00:54:58.600
I can see the passion
oozing out as you talk.

00:54:58.600 --> 00:55:01.440
So I think we're just at time.

00:55:01.440 --> 00:55:03.410
So I wanted to thank everyone--

00:55:03.410 --> 00:55:08.220
Clémence, Josh, the folks
from the Open Learning Office,

00:55:08.220 --> 00:55:11.310
the folks at MITili
and MIT more generally.

00:55:11.310 --> 00:55:13.370
Thank you all for joining
in and participating

00:55:13.370 --> 00:55:15.000
and for your questions.

00:55:15.000 --> 00:55:18.040
And that's a wrap.

00:55:18.040 --> 00:55:20.090
Thanks, everyone.

